## P18-V01 `queryConsumeTimeSpan` admin RPC — 2026-10-18T02:53:02Z

- Reconciled Java `MqClientAdminImpl.queryConsumeTimeSpan` (request 303) and
  added an invoked fixture for the raw address, timeout, inherited header
  fields, result body/list, and Broker exception. Rust's existing Core RPC now
  sends to the provided address directly; Push Consumer applies VIP mapping
  before the call to preserve its Java facade behavior. Added a VIP-enabled
  Core direct-address regression and a fixture-driven Core RPC test.
- Code commit: `72a37b17` (`feat(admin): reconcile query consume time span RPC`).
- First fixture invocation used the fixed `compat.Main` entry point and failed
  its usage check; added a Main dispatcher and regenerated through it. Initial
  Core test compilation exposed a fixture-field type mismatch and local API
  binding shadowing; corrected. The first Broker-error assertion showed the
  scripted peer helper discarded remarks; added a remark-capable helper and
  the comparison now passes.
- Passed: Core query-time-span unit tests (2), Core `admin_rpc` (29), focused
  Consumer VIP routing test, strict all-target/all-feature Clippy for Core and
  Consumer, `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `git diff --check`, and Maven
  `verify` (37 scenarios, fixture checks, 60 typed-header and 14 typed-body
  cross-decodes). The Java fixture is
  `fixtures/admin/query-consume-time-span.json` (SHA-256
  `5689eea4d53809d6ce2caa6bebc14ba4ebeb8b49b8fffe3000432bbe73dfaa4e`).
- Updated Phase 17's admin matrix to 5/19 source-backed methods and appended
  this evidence to the release checklist. Remaining 14 `MqClientAdmin` methods
  and broad P18-V01 remain open; full-workspace P18-V02, Ubuntu/native Windows
  P18-V04, final P18-V03 package/provenance review and local Phase 19 matrix
  remain pending. Existing Phase 19 worktree changes remain unstaged.
